  "dimensions": "123 × 93.9 cm",
  "production_date": "probably 1620-5",
  "brief_description": "The infant Christ is the central figure in this painting, but he is the only one who doesn't look at us directly. Gripping his rosary (the Catholic prayer beads used to keep count of devotions), he gazes beyond the frame in contemplation, while Joseph, the Virgin Mary and the young John the Baptist stare forward.\n\nJordaens likely painted this picture with Caravaggio's Madonna of the Rosary in mind (the picture had travelled from Naples to one of Antwerp's many Catholic churches in the early seventeenth century). The rosary beads resurface here and, combined with Christ's outward gaze, they might hint at something beyond his childhood. The reed cross held by John stands in for the crucifix of the Passion (Christ's torture and crucifixion), which was contemplated by means of the rosary prayer. Joseph, Mary and John address us, but Christ addresses his future.",
  "accession_date": "1838-01-01",
